# Netlib Kennington subset — NOT YET PINNED. See PLAN 2.8.2 and Q6.
#
# Sixteen larger problems from Carolan, Hill, Kennington, Niemi and Wichmann,
# "An Empirical Evaluation of the KORBX Algorithms for Military Airlift
# Applications", Operations Research 38(2), 1990, 240-248. The M1 gate wants
# them for correctness only, with no performance expectation (PLAN 2.9).
#
# Run with:  build/bench/run -m bench/netlib-kennington.manifest
#
# Same blocker as the infeasible set. netlib.org/lp/data/kennington/ carries
# these as .gz in packed emps form (verified 2026-08-07 by fetching ken-07.gz
# and reading its header after gunzip: packed, not MPS). Koch's mirror does
# not carry them. Q6 has to be decided before this file can be filled.
#
# Format is the standard manifest's:
#
#     name  sha256  rows  cols  reference  source  objconst
#
# The 16 instances:
#
#   cre-a   cre-b   cre-c   cre-d
#   ken-07  ken-11  ken-13  ken-18
#   osa-07  osa-14  osa-30  osa-60
#   pds-02  pds-06  pds-10  pds-20
#
# Reference values are an open sub-question: whether Koch's exact rationals
# cover these is unverified. If they do not, the fallback is the netlib
# readme's PROBLEM SUMMARY TABLE, marked `netlib` in the source column like
# any other — and read in the light of pilot87, where exactly that fallback
# turned out to be outside the gate's own tolerance (bench/README.md).
#
# These are the largest instances JAOS will have loaded. Some carry tens of
# thousands of columns, so this set is also the first real test of whether
# anything in the readers or the factorization scales past the standard set.
